<div class=3D"section" id=3D"s4">
  <div class=3D"section-header purple">
    <div class=3D"section-num">04</div>
    <div class=3D"section-header-text">
      <h2>Create the Course Project &amp; Virtual Environment</h2>
      <p>A Poetry project automatically sets up and manages the virtual env=
ironment for you.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>Create a project folder for this course. We call it <code>agentic-ai-c=
ourse</code>.</p>

  <div class=3D"code-block">
    <div class=3D"code-header">
      <span class=3D"code-lang">bash</span>
      <span class=3D"code-label">Create project with Poetry</span>
      <button class=3D"copy-btn">Copy</button>
    </div>
    <pre><span class=3D"cm"># Go to wherever you keep your projects</span>
cd ~/projects          <span class=3D"cm"># macOS / Linux</span>
<span class=3D"cm"># cd C:\Users\YourName\projects   =E2=86=90 Windows</spa=
n>

<span class=3D"cm"># Create the project</span>
poetry new agentic-ai-course
cd agentic-ai-course

<span class=3D"cm"># Open in your IDE</span>
cursor .    <span class=3D"cm"># or: code .  for VS Code</span></pre>
  </div>

  <p>Poetry creates this structure:</p>

  <div class=3D"file-tree">
    <span class=3D"dir">agentic-ai-course/</span><br>
    =E2=94=9C=E2=94=80=E2=94=80 <span class=3D"dir">agentic_ai_course/</spa=
n>   <span class=3D"note">=E2=86=90 your Python package (we'll add scripts =
here)</span><br>
    =E2=94=82&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;=E2=94=94=E2=94=80=E2=94=80 __init__.py<br>
    =E2=94=9C=E2=94=80=E2=94=80 <span class=3D"dir">tests/</span><br>
    =E2=94=82&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;=E2=94=94=E2=94=80=E2=94=80 __init__.py<br>
    =E2=94=9C=E2=94=80=E2=94=80 pyproject.toml       <span class=3D"note">=
=E2=86=90 project config &amp; dependencies</span><br>
    =E2=94=94=E2=94=80=E2=94=80 README.md
  </div>

  <h3 class=3D"sub">Understanding the Virtual Environment</h3>
  <p>Because you ran <code>poetry config virtualenvs.in-project true</code>=
 in the previous step, Poetry will create the virtual environment inside th=
e project folder as <code>.venv/</code> the first time you run <code>poetry=
 install</code>. This makes it easy for your IDE to detect it automatically=
.</p>

  <div class=3D"code-block">
    <div class=3D"code-header">
      <span class=3D"code-lang">bash</span>
      <span class=3D"code-label">Verify the virtual environment</span>
      <button class=3D"copy-btn">Copy</button>
    </div>
    <pre><span class=3D"cm"># Install the project (creates the .venv/ folde=
r)</span>
poetry install

<span class=3D"cm"># Check where the venv lives</span>
poetry env info

<span class=3D"cm"># Activate the venv in your terminal (optional =E2=80=94=
 poetry run works without this)</span>
poetry shell

<span class=3D"cm"># You should now see (.venv) at the start of your prompt=
</span>
<span class=3D"cm"># Verify Python inside the venv</span>
python --version
<span class=3D"cm"># =E2=86=92 Python 3.12.x  (from the venv, not your syst=
em Python)</span></pre>
  </div>

  <div class=3D"callout blue">
    <div class=3D"callout-title">IDE: Point it to the .venv</div>
    In Cursor or VS Code: press <strong>Cmd/Ctrl + Shift + P</strong> =E2=
=86=92 "Python: Select Interpreter" =E2=86=92 choose the interpreter inside=
 <code>.venv/</code>. This gives you correct autocomplete and type checking=
 for all installed packages.
  </div>
</div>

